\title{Solving problems with dynamic programming}
\date{2026-10-28T13:13:46Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{In general, here are the steps you should follow to solve a problem with dynamic programming:

\ol{
  \li{Try something else first! (Greedy, reduce to standard graph algorithm, ...)}
  \li{Come up with a recurrence.  This is the hardest part!  It does get easier with experience.
  \ul{
    \li{Optional: prove the recurrence correct via induction.}
  }
  }
  \li{Check: do the recursive subproblems overlap?
  \ul{
    \li{No: rejoice!  You have a [divide & conquer](00PJ) algorithm.}
    \li{Yes: proceed with the rest of the steps!}
  }
  }
  \li{[Memoize](00QJ) the recurrence, either by [adding a table to the recursive algorithm](00QK), or by [directly filling in a table with some loop(s)](00QL).  Note that it is also possible to memoize with more exotic data structures (dictionaries, lazy infinite trees, ...) but in this class we will only see examples using arrays!}
  \li{Optional: to reconstruct an optimal solution, remember the choices made along the way, then reconstruct the solution in reverse.  (We'll see examples of this later.)}
}

}
